"""Recursive construction walk vs iterative walker — the issue's tooth.

Models the scenario: two entrances naively use a recursive walk that blows
the stack on deep trees; the census entrance (the truthful one) does not.
The fix shape is to lift the recursive descent onto a walker using an
explicit cycle policy (visited set + depth cap) so that:

  * deep construction no longer depends on Python's call stack,
  * a node re-entering its own construction is reported as a CYCLE defect
    rather than a RecursionError (frame names cannot separate those, but
    coordinates can),
  * traversal order is deterministic-by-construction.

Two walkers demonstrate the contrast:
  * RecursiveAssembler — hand-rolled recursion (the crutch shape),
  * IterativeAssembler   — walker over typed Child edges with visited set.

Both walk the same tree; only the iterative one survives a depth that
exceeds the (simulated) default recursion budget.
"""

